# Push targets and PR safety

There are three repositories to keep straight:

- **`origin`** — your personal fork (e.g. `https://github.com/<your-handle>/excalidraw.git`). This is what you cloned and this is where your branches get pushed.
- **`jgrace19/excalidraw`** — the workshop fork. Pull requests target this repo's default branch. You do **not** push branches directly to it.
- **`excalidraw/excalidraw`** — the true upstream Excalidraw project. Off-limits for workshop work; do not push and do not open PRs against it.

Never push directly to a default branch (`main`, `master`, `trunk`, `develop`) on any of these remotes. Always use a feature branch and open a PR.

## Required

- Push branches only to `origin` (your own fork). Verify with `git remote -v` before pushing if you are not sure.
- Open all PRs with `jgrace19/excalidraw` as the **base** and your fork's branch as the **head** — not against upstream `excalidraw/excalidraw`.
- Never push directly to `main`, `master`, or any default branch on any remote. Always use a feature branch.

## Verifying before pushing

Run the following before any push:

```
git remote -v
git branch --show-current
```

- The `origin` URL should match `https://github.com/<your-handle>/excalidraw.git` (or the SSH equivalent). If it points at `jgrace19/excalidraw` or `excalidraw/excalidraw`, stop — you cloned the wrong repo. Fork first, then clone your fork.
- The current branch should not be `main` or `master`.

If a second remote (e.g. an `upstream` pointing at `jgrace19/excalidraw` or `excalidraw/excalidraw`) is added, push explicitly with the remote name to avoid ambiguity:

```
git push origin <branch-name>
```

## Opening a PR

Use the GitHub UI or `gh pr create` with an explicit `--repo` flag so the PR is filed against the workshop fork, not your own fork or upstream:

```
gh pr create --repo jgrace19/excalidraw --base master --head <your-handle>:<branch-name>
```

Without `--repo`, `gh` may infer the wrong target — particularly if an `upstream` remote is configured or if you forgot to specify the head branch on your fork.

## If you have committed directly on a default branch

Do not push. Move the commit to a feature branch first:

```
git switch -c <feature-branch>
git switch master
git reset --hard origin/master
git switch <feature-branch>
git push -u origin <feature-branch>
```

Then open a PR.

## If you have already pushed somewhere wrong

- **Pushed to upstream `excalidraw/excalidraw`:** stop and tell the user. Do not force-push or delete the remote branch without explicit confirmation.
- **Pushed to `jgrace19/excalidraw`:** stop and tell the user. You likely have write access only to your own fork, so this is uncommon, but if a branch landed there by mistake, do not delete or force-push it without confirmation.
- **Pushed to a default branch on your own fork:** stop and tell the user. Offer to revert via a follow-up PR.

## Exception

Only push directly to a default branch, or to any repo other than your own fork, when the user explicitly authorizes that specific operation. A generic "commit and push" never grants this permission — interpret it as "commit on a feature branch on your fork and open a PR against `jgrace19/excalidraw`."
